---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import BlogPostHeader from '../../components/BlogPostHeader.astro';

const title = "Fake Airport PCN Payment Sites: How to Spot Them | AppealAirportPCN";
const description = "Learn how to spot unofficial airport drop-off payment sites, fake PCN texts and emails, and what to do if you have already paid one.";
const canonical = "https://appealairportpcn.co.uk/guides/fake-airport-pcn-payment-sites/";

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Article",
  "headline": "Fake airport PCN payment sites: how to spot them",
  "description": description,
  "author": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "AppealAirportPCN.co.uk" }
};
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical={canonical} schema={schema}>
  <BlogPostHeader
    title="Fake airport PCN payment sites: how to spot them"
    breadcrumbLabel="Fake payment sites"
    breadcrumbHref="/guides/fake-airport-pcn-payment-sites/"
    updatedDate="October 2026"
    fixedUpdatedDate={true}
  />
  <main>
    <div class="wrap">
      <article class="blog-post">
        <p>You search "Heathrow drop-off charge pay". The top result looks official. You pay in a rush. Later you find out it wasn't the airport's site.</p>
        <p>This happens a lot. Here is how these sites work, and how to avoid them.</p>

        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <p>Only pay on the airport's own website. Type the address yourself. Don't click an ad.</p>
        <p>Don't pay anyone to appeal a PCN for you. Appeals are free. Our templates are free too.</p>

        <h2>Why this works on people</h2>
        <p>The charge is due fast. You're often at the airport, on your phone, in a hurry.</p>
        <p>Search ads can sit above the real result. Look-alike sites copy the airport's colours and wording.</p>
        <p>Trading Standards teams have warned about this for years. A search engine is far more likely to show you copycat sites than the real one.</p>

        <h2>Type 1: "We'll pay it for you" sites</h2>
        <p>These sites look like the official payment page. You enter your details. They add a "service fee". Then they pay the airport for you, or say they do.</p>

        <p><strong>Real examples.</strong> Trustpilot reviewers describe two sites, <em>heathrowdropoffcharge.co.uk</em> and <em>gatwickdropoffcharge.co.uk</em>. We are not linking to them.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>At Heathrow, reviewers say the site added a £4 "service fee" to the £6 charge. One says it was not recognised by Heathrow. Several say it came up first on Google.</li>
          <li>At Gatwick, a reviewer says they were asked for £10 instead of £6. They said the £4 fee covered a text confirmation and "error checking". They say they had dropped off in four minutes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These are reviews from 2025, when the charges were lower. We can't check every claim. And the sites may have changed since. But the pattern is clear: they aren't the airport's own site, and they add a fee.</p>

        <p><strong>Is it legal?</strong> It depends. Trading Standards say it isn't illegal to offer a paying service, if the business is upfront about who it is and what it costs. What they can't do is mislead you into thinking they are the official site.</p>
        <p>Trading Standards also say these sites often have a disclaimer and a link to the official site. It may be in small print you don't notice.</p>

        <h2>Type 2: Sites that don't pay at all</h2>
        <p>Some unofficial sites take your money and don't pass it on. Bath &amp; North East Somerset Council has warned about this for Clean Air Zone charges. It says people who paid through unofficial sites could still get a PCN, because the site failed to pay on their behalf.</p>
        <p>The same risk applies to any middleman. If you pay them and they don't pay the airport, you still owe the airport. And you may get a PCN.</p>
        <p>If you paid a third party, check with the airport that your payment arrived.</p>

        <h2>Type 3: Fake texts and emails</h2>
        <p>Councils across the UK have warned about fake texts in 2026. They say you owe a parking fine, and they link to a fake payment page.</p>
        <p>These messages usually:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Say they're from "a local council" with no other detail.</li>
          <li>Threaten court action, a bigger fine or a credit score hit.</li>
          <li>Ask for payment right away.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Councils say they would never send a PCN by text. A real notice gives your car details, the place, the date and the time. A scam text doesn't.</p>
        <p>Fake emails have used real parking company names too. UKPC warned about one in the past.</p>
        <p>Airport PCNs are private charges, not council fines. But scammers use the same trick. If a message is vague, or you don't remember being there, don't click.</p>

        <h2>How to spot a fake</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>It's an ad.</strong> Look for "Ad" or "Sponsored" above the result.</li>
          <li><strong>The address isn't the airport's.</strong> Check the web address before you enter anything. Heathrow's is heathrow.com/dropoff.</li>
          <li><strong>There's a "service fee", "admin fee" or "processing fee".</strong> The airport doesn't charge you extra to pay online.</li>
          <li><strong>The total is higher than the airport's price.</strong> Check the charge on the airport's own site first.</li>
          <li><strong>It says it will "deal with your PCN".</strong> Be careful. You don't need to pay anyone for that.</li>
          <li><strong>A text or email arrived out of the blue.</strong> Don't click it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Where to pay properly</h2>
        <p>Go to the airport's own website. Type the address in yourself, or go to the airport's main site and follow the drop-off charge link.</p>
        <p>Airports don't let you pay in person at the drop-off zone. You pay online or by phone. The RAC says this about Heathrow and Gatwick.</p>

        <h2>If you already paid a fake site</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Keep every receipt and screenshot.</li>
          <li>Check with the airport that your payment arrived.</li>
          <li>Contact your bank or card provider. Ask if you can dispute the payment.</li>
          <li>Report it to Citizens Advice's consumer service, which passes it to Trading Standards.</li>
          <li>If it was a fake text or email, forward the text to 7726. Report it to Action Fraud.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Our view</h2>
        <p>We think airport drop-off charges are unfair. Dropping someone off shouldn't cost a fortune, and it shouldn't be a business opportunity.</p>
        <p>So we think help with these charges should be free. That includes our templates and guides. You shouldn't pay a stranger to write an appeal.</p>
        <p>Appealing a private parking charge is free. Citizens Advice says so. It also says that paying is usually seen as admitting the ticket was right, so you often can't appeal after you've paid.</p>
        <p>If you got a PCN, <a href="/">pick your airport and use our free template</a>. If you want to avoid a charge in the first place, use the airport's own site, and pay on time.</p>
        <p>The only way we ask for support is an optional tip jar. The templates are always free.</p>

        <div class="disclaimer">
          <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> This is general information, not legal advice.
        </div>

